.TH BACK 6.SH NAMEback \- OS-9 backgammon on a full-screen board.SH SYNOPSIS\fBback\fR.SH DESCRIPTIONBackgammon against the computer, or between two players, on a board drawnacross the whole screen: the points numbered 1 to 24, the dice cup andthe doubling status beside it, and a list of commands.  Each command isone letter:.TP 12\fBR\fRroll the dice, then make your moves..TP 12\fBD\fRdouble the stakes..TP 12\fBS\fRshow the last move again..TP 12\fBC\fRclear and redraw the screen..TP 12\fBN\fRbegin a new game..TP 12\fBH\fRhelp..TP 12\fB$\fRrun an OS-9 shell and come back..TP 12\fBQ\fRquit, after asking..PPIts screen codes are a TeleVideo's, whatever TERM says: ESC = and a rowand column to move the cursor, ESC * to clear the screen..SH NOTESNeeds Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fRThe\fB$\fRcommand forks a module called\fBshell\fR,\fRwhich is the one your own OS-9 brings..SH SEE ALSObackgammon, teachgammon